  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 451 PM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nMain change to the forecast was to increase rain chances Wednesday \nafternoon-early evening for areas north of US 24, with the best \npotential closer to Lake MI. Models have shifted southeastward with \nthe precipitation from previous runs, with a weak shortwave riding \nthe ridge stalled to our southeast. Simultaneously, moisture return \nahead of Polo remnants in the southwest and return flow around the \nhigh coming off the Gulf take aim right into our northwest late \ntonight into Wed, providing enough moisture/lift for precipitation. \nInstability is still limited at the surface, with weak mid-level \nlapse rates, so kept any thunder mention out for this period. Highs \nnorth of US 24 will be in the 60s, low 70s. South of US 24, expect \nmainly increasing clouds and highs in the mid-upper 70s/low 80s.  \n\nThere could be a lull in precipitation after 8pm EDT as the wave \nexits (subsidence) and moisture transport slows before the LLJ kicks \nin again. Otherwise, we remain on the ridge/trough boundary and \ncontinued moisture transport/warm air advection through Friday \nmorning, when the frontal boundary and upper trough finally press \nsoutheastward, leaving us with some breezy northwest winds and a \ngradual drying trend into the afternoon. Gradually, pops will expand \nsoutheast Thursday afternoon-with much of the area in 60-90% chances \ninto Friday morning. Highs on Thursday look to be in the low-mid 70s \nnorth of US 24, and the upper 70s, low 80s further south. Behind the \nfront on Friday, highs will be in the 60s. \n\nA surface high builds in Friday afternoon into Sunday morning, \nbringing a return to dry conditions. It will be cooler, with highs \nin the 60s, low 70s. Flow aloft is slightly cyclonic or more zonal \nduring this time, with another wave (and surface low) drifting \nacross northern Lower and Upper MI Sunday morning, then drifting \ninto Ontario/Quebec by Sunday night. Most of the guidance keeps our \narea dry for now, with the better forcing/precip chances further \nnorth into MI. Troughing lingers aloft until Monday evening when \nanother upper ridge builds in with surface high pressure sprawling \nacross the GL through mid next week. High temperatures will remain \nlargely in the 60s, with lows in the 40s. It's possible we see \nwidespread lows in the upper 30s, low 40s on Monday night \n(especially in MI counties).",
